\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-2+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}-[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha