uestion/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-3+2\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bb604fd16125fd3998071ecdad93c8fe8f058039da92f0ee65999b5bfaf0e16f"} {"question" "q-334226" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334226" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334226" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(2+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(2+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}(3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}(3{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-2[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+0[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334224" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bb604fd16125fd3998071ecdad93c8fe8f058039da92f0ee65999b5bfaf0e16f"} {"question" "q-334224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334224" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+0[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bb604fd16125fd3998071ecdad93c8fe8f058039da92f0ee65999b5bfaf0e16f"} {"question" "q-334225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] ["math" "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "q-334225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\text{x}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/state.json" "file_sha256" "0e3191812156f778cf041de5e56cf3304a8e6b4429721951f136737307b01287"} {"question" "e-7043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/1ee7ef0a95e618121f4e0653c6beaa64248682ab524ecee8903acec89827e77d.png)\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/031dd658d772ef4184ceb173311102f64bd0ff3ff5e34321ad4648e0d5026383.png)\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/d82f994f6426a091fb6fc46b2b2b6fbc66d0b94e7ed1ead3e67257292f4c985b.png)*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/example-7043.json" "file_sha256" "4e7d8eda80836d6276d059142ee744752a624917870b26d26bc7074369dfa34e"} {"question" "e-7043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/b1ba76ba62019524549b4bcf4c36ec2440b2db8f585150a9456218972363a215.png) \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/f86bdf3e241a51548f8c58fe739e11071197944f5fe2361f83bb34408333211c.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/9b2628d7d3c88303bdd7ed2a8aadcdcfe751a01d910f64972a60cc080d039181.png)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = \\cos{t} + \\sin{t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/example-7043.json" "file_sha256" "4e7d8eda80836d6276d059142ee744752a624917870b26d26bc7074369dfa34e"} {"question" "e-7044" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/4d932848b1d30448dd0135bc75ded935f2065037d4964423899013a47c7a526a.png)\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/a474b7882cfa32e343d1781a731a4eb540994f72730320d7b8a61639e7e86a14.png)*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/example-7044.json" "file_sha256" "978acdc4f5291b43953776902c155661f6e00297e3e12d25485835d82d83cfed"} {"question" "e-7044" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/60dd7b0b483c745b0ffda2c89d3216ab463cab5870f91b58b54c7f8e4e446cfd.png)\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/9e4e860107360ac18ce72e7f4f07dbf9106de041dea3b6c9228cc30bc45c714e.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/175d9bde8dda1bfb3a653521add06d23944d72019c0718eed6407abe97f2e87c.png)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/12f33df5f036901c6069ee03a9ba8ccb05cde1071b7add3d1b310da12bb8298d.png) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/example-7044.json" "file_sha256" "978acdc4f5291b43953776902c155661f6e00297e3e12d25485835d82d83cfed"} {"question" "e-7045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/ae6acacc8b2c54be234e536c373c40f4b5b53646fe6091181672c06e437f5366.png)\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/14c8e142a4fc541b8da91c6bcd1c8517451b436b7f8c677bfb239ca4bfa79588.png)\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/example-7045.json" "file_sha256" "6a9961e1ae0bab22f3940538477e5fffe6b3d49b86283e08b82fc7547c4c672d"} {"question" "e-7045" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/471ff0aa856363c49a494f0e711dec8558b429261ddd99685bd0a19416264d05.png) \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For $\\lambda_1 =1+\\textrm{i},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/9fbccda402b330b568828c47040f1867c0b075ff434f6d94c2d5d5bcea85f4a8.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/91e10c00aed02305fdb5afd421aad57fe52143e3305d84da00819e62f2b30a14.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 1+\\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/2ccc9c4cb7dd95e168690c017e8fca6f92315d1759b3e3ec6bb3a82551b39e3c.png)\n\nWe now separate this solution into its real and imaginary parts. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ } \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t) = \\cos{t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/example-7045.json" "file_sha256" "6a9961e1ae0bab22f3940538477e5fffe6b3d49b86283e08b82fc7547c4c672d"} {"question" "e-7046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/082bb18706b8bae20dbc5b8531829f3b8523d9262c2fd0f7c4be78a3b2ef4ebb.png)\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/example-7046.json" "file_sha256" "e1f75fcef9a6a2d800f00f74a7c59a7527d3756bac5b958d5ab760b088b9fe28"} {"question" "e-7046" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/657dced55da3afc2c15487267793aaf282e8c258ca5e7556bacecff58a496d82.png) \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For $\\lambda_1 =3+\\textrm{i},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/9fbccda402b330b568828c47040f1867c0b075ff434f6d94c2d5d5bcea85f4a8.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/5d9c13b213c1e44a755000997c61f5ba5a3c91159eb7a5e6a574acf34019efab.png)\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1$ is \n$\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 3 + \\textrm{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/38fd775722fd8ce56abb0f09499bb0a268ae73b9483a182d4564381557d190cf.png)\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/assets/f2282999a9f221bd9a14118c116c573f82521e6140d24b008b6fea04c5a61fcf.png) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068029/example-7046.json" "file_sha256" "e1f75fcef9a6a2d800f00f74a7c59a7527d3756bac5b958d5ab760b088b9fe28"}] "usage" {"q-329578" [] "e-7043" [] "q-328595" [] "e-7045" [] "e-7044" [] "e-7046" [] "q-328592" []} "basis" 2273 "retractions" [[17592186157680 :question/problem "$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n$x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $λ_{1} = 2\\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector $v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.*"] [17592186157687 :question/problem "$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$.\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n$x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}g(t) \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $λ_{1} = \\textrm{i}$.*\n\n$f(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n$g(t) =$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186157683 :question/problem "$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n$x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cos (2t) \\\\ \\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $λ_{1} = 1 + 2\\textrm{i}$.*"] [17592186157002 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$*"] [17592186157002 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1\n$$\n\nBut we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand its associated eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}e^{\\textrm{i}t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\cos t + \\textrm{i}\\sin t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t + \\textrm{i}\\sin t + \\textrm{i}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t + 2\\textrm{i}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(\\cos t - \\sin t) + \\textrm{i}(\\cos t + \\sin t) \\\\ 2\\cos t + 2\\textrm{i}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t\\end{bmatrix} + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t + \\sin t \\\\ 2\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= w_{1}(t) + \\textrm{i}w_{2}(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}w_{1}(t) + c_{2}w_{2}(t) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t + \\sin t \\\\ 2\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\cos{t} + \\sin{t}\n$$"] [17592186157003 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$*"] [17592186157003 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1\n$$\n\nBut we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand its associated eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2\\textrm{i}t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}(\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\cos (2t) - \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(\\cos (2t) - \\sin (2t)) + \\textrm{i}(\\cos (2t) + \\sin (2t)) \\\\ \\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) - \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix} + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= w_{1}(t) + \\textrm{i}w_{2}(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}w_{1}(t) + c_{2}w_{2}(t) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) - \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\ c_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 1\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) - \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\cos (2t) - 3\\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157004 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}$.*"] [17592186157004 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 =1+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwe get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1\n$$\n\nBut we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 1+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand its associated eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe now separate this solution into its real and imaginary parts. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{(1 + \\textrm{i})t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}(\\cos t + \\textrm{i}\\sin t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i}(e^{t}\\cos t) - e^{t}\\sin t \\\\ e^{t}\\cos t + \\textrm{i}(e^{t}\\sin t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\\n&= w_{1}(t) + \\textrm{i}w_{2}(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}w_{1}(t) + c_{2}w_{2}(t) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\cos{t}\n$$"] [17592186157005 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}$.*"] [17592186157005 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 =3+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwe get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1\n$$\n\nBut we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 3 + \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand its associated eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{(3 + \\textrm{i})t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}(\\cos t + \\textrm{i}\\sin t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i}(e^{3t}\\cos t) - e^{3t}\\sin t \\\\ e^{3t}\\cos t + \\textrm{i}(e^{3t}\\sin t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} \\\\\n&= w_{1}(t) + \\textrm{i}w_{2}(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}w_{1}(t) + c_{2}w_{2}(t) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}c_{2} \\\\ c_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 1\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t - 3\\cos t \\\\ \\cos t - 3\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186155250 17592186155251 17592186155252 17592186155253 17592186155254 17592186155268 17592186155269 17592186155270 17592186155271 17592186155272] "immutable_fields" [[17592186155249 :answer-field/correct] [17592186155249 :answer-field/id] [17592186155249 :answer-field/key] [17592186155249 :answer-field/type] [17592186155267 :answer-field/correct] [17592186155267 :answer-field/id] [17592186155267 :answer-field/key] [17592186155267 :answer-field/type] [17592186155291 :answer-field/id] [17592186155291 :answer-field/key] [17592186155291 :answer-field/type] [17592186155292 :answer-field/id] [17592186155292 :answer-field/key] [17592186155292 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
